<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm at the Market. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at the Market.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe routines and simple procedures. Interactive: Participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe routines using location phrases (e.g., "I go to school in the morning."). Can participate in dialogue and answer questions about locations using simple terms (e.g., "Where are you?" "I'm at the market."). Can describe personal experiences by asking questions about familiar places (e.g., "Are you at school?" "I'm at school.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different places (home, school, market, park, library, store). The teacher says the name of each place clearly, and students repeat chorally. "This is the market. Market!" (focusing on /m/). "This is school. School!" (focusing on /sk/). Then, the teacher asks, "Where are you?" and points to different students, encouraging them to say "I'm at..." if they know, or repeating the phrase for them. The teacher also shows a picture of the market and asks, "Where is this place?" to elicit "Market!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "home" and says, "I'm at home." Then, the teacher holds up "school" and says, "I'm at school." The teacher models the question "Are you at school?" and answers "Yes, I'm at school." The teacher emphasizes the contraction "I'm" and the preposition "at." The teacher also models "Thank you!" when appropriate for polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the "market" flashcard: "Am I at school or at the market?" (Expected answer: "Market!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a student: "Are you at home or at school right now?" (Expected answer: "School!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue displayed on a whiteboard or large poster. The dialogue features two children (e.g., Ana and Tom) asking each other "Where are you?" and stating their locations using "I'm at the..." and "I'm at home/school." It includes the market as a key location.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><u>Example Dialogue Snippet:</u><br>
                    <b>Ana:</b> Hi, Tom! Where are you?<br>
                    <b>Tom:</b> Hi, Ana! I'm at the market. It's busy here.<br>
                    <b>Ana:</b> Oh! Are you at the market? I'm at the park. I play here.<br>
                    <b>Tom:</b> That's nice! See you later!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics and describe locations using "I'm at..." and "Are you at...?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to the pictures. Then, students repeat. The teacher asks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is Tom?" (Expected answer: "At the market!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Ana at the market?" (Expected answer: "No, she isn't. She's at the park.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using contractions ("I'm"), prepositions of place ("at"), and the present simple ("I'm at school.") to describe and confirm locations. They will use vocabulary for places (home, school, market, park, library, store, hospital, pharmacy, bakery, restaurant) and simple verbs (go, play, buy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of different places and incomplete sentences (e.g., Picture of a park: "I'm ___ the ______."). Students verbally complete the sentences in pairs or small groups. The teacher circulates, checking pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ and correct use of "at." Example: "I'm at the park."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Picture Cue Drill:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of places (e.g., market, school, hospital) rapidly. Students respond chorally and then individually with "I'm at the [place]." The teacher then introduces simple actions for each place (e.g., market: "I buy fruit at the market."; school: "I study at school."). Students practice saying "I'm at the market. I buy fruit."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Guess the Place:</b> Students work in pairs. One student chooses a flashcard of a place (without showing it) and says, "I'm at the [place]." The other student asks, "Are you at the [different place]?" until they guess correctly. Example:
                        <br>Student A: "I'm at the market."
                        <br>Student B: "Are you at school?"
                        <br>Student A: "No, I'm not."
                        <br>Student B: "Are you at the market?"
                        <br>Student A: "Yes, I'm at the market! Good guess! Thank you!"</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair Role-Play: "Where are you, friend?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive small cards with pictures of different places (e.g., one student gets 'market', another gets 'school', another 'park'). They should keep their card hidden.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students imagine they are calling a friend. Student A starts by asking, "Hi [Friend's Name]! Where are you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B responds based on their card, "Hi [Friend's Name]! I'm at the [place on card]." (e.g., "I'm at the market.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A then asks, "Are you at the [place on THEIR card]?" (e.g., if Student A has 'park', "Are you at the park?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B answers, "No, I'm not." or "Yes, I'm at the [place]! Thank you for asking!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students switch roles and repeat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to use polite expressions like "Excuse me," "Please," and "Thank you" in their interactions. For example, if one student describes their location, the other can respond, "Your market sounds busy! Thank you!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in describing and confirming locations, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to use the pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ correctly.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the pair role-play. The teacher listens to pairs and notes their use of target language and interaction skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Where are you, friend?" pair role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target words (e.g., /sk/ in 'school', /m/ in 'market').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "I'm" and "at" for locations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3 target vocabulary words for places (e.g., market, school, home, park).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and answer "Where are you?" questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of polite expressions (e.g., "Thank you!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and clear communication with partner.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick oral or written reflection before leaving class:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new place you can say in English."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned to say today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to ask 'Where are you?' to your friend today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite place to say in English, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The pair role-play activity had good engagement and allowed for repeated practice of the target structures. Students generally picked up "I'm at the..." and "Are you at...?" quickly with the visual cues.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with the pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ consistently; more focused drills might be beneficial. Also, encourage more spontaneous use of polite expressions. Perhaps providing a small dialogue frame during the role-play could help ensure all target language is used.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This speaking lesson has solidified the oral production of locations and simple routines. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on this by having students write simple sentences and paragraphs describing where they go and what they do, reinforcing the vocabulary and grammar learned today in a written format.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>